---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../components/ui/Container.astro";
import { Content as SetupGuide } from "../../components/nightwatch/SetupGuide.md";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Nightwatch: a red display for your iPhone"
  description="Set up a red display across your iPhone with iOS Color Filters, an Accessibility Shortcut, and optional Shortcuts automations."
  image="/og/nightwatch.png"
>
  <Container class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/apps/" class="text-sm">
        ← Apps
      </a>
      <div class="mt-8 grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto]">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Setup guide · iOS only</p>
          <h1 class="mb-6">Nightwatch</h1>
          <p class="text-lead mb-6">A red display for the night watch.</p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted">
            An app’s dark theme stops at its edges. iOS Color Filters can tint
            your whole display red, including other apps and the lock screen.
            These instructions use settings already on your iPhone. There’s no
            Nightwatch app to install.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="mx-auto max-w-64">
          <img
            src="/og/nightwatch.png"
            width="850"
            height="1850"
            alt="Illustration of Slackwater with a red tint across the entire interface."
            class="h-auto w-full rounded-3xl"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-fg-faint mt-3 text-sm">
            Red-tint illustration. iOS screenshots don’t capture the display
            filter.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg mt-16 max-w-none">
        <SetupGuide />

        <h2>Making this easier</h2>
        <p>
          We’re trying to turn this setup into a Nightwatch app that makes
          configuration, controls, and troubleshooting easier. We’ve asked Apple
          to add the APIs we need to manage the system-wide red tint directly.
          For now, iOS settings and Shortcuts are the way to do it, and you can
          use this guide without waiting for an app.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .prose :global(.setup-screenshots) {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-block: 2rem;
  }
  .prose :global(.setup-screenshots figure) {
    flex: 1 1 16rem;
    max-width: 20rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .prose :global(table) {
    display: block;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .prose :global(td) {
    min-width: 12rem;
  }
  .prose :global(img) {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 1rem;
  }
  .prose :global(pre) {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
